      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1004543002,
      "version": 700,
      "versionNonce": 1466499654,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1738865442027,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Pointers: address --> 64bit or 8bytes (CPU architecture is 64bit)\n\nin C language, we have pointer arithmetic which means, we can have p++ or p--\nbut in Go, we have static pointers and we can do arithmetic operations on Go\npointers (unless we use unsafe package to do this because it is not a safe statement).\n\nIf we have a pointer to primitive data types, the pointer will point to an address in stack\n(stack to stack), but if we have a pointer to concrete types (structs, maps, slices and ...),\nthe pointer will point to an address in heap (stack to heap).\n \n\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Pointers: address --> 64bit or 8bytes (CPU architecture is 64bit)\n\nin C language, we have pointer arithmetic which means, we can have p++ or p--\nbut in Go, we have static pointers and we can do arithmetic operations on Go\npointers (unless we use unsafe package to do this because it is not a safe statement).\n\nIf we have a pointer to primitive data types, the pointer will point to an address in stack\n(stack to stack), but if we have a pointer to concrete types (structs, maps, slices and ...),\nthe pointer will point to an address in heap (stack to heap).\n \n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
